nce record, with refresher date          | SOP 9-02        | Proposed                              |\n| CAP-F04  | Exercise plan                                                | SOP 9-03        | Proposed; protocol runs in drill mode |\n| CAP-F05  | IEC programme, reach log and sector consultation record      | SOPs 9-04, 9-09 | Proposed                              |\n| CAP-F06  | BDRRMC functionality matrix (85 barangays)                   | SOP 9-06        | Proposed                              |\n| CAP-F07  | Purok welfare-check time record                              | SOPs 9-03, 9-06 | Proposed                              |\n| CAP-F08  | Volunteer registry entry                                     | SOP 9-07        | Proposed; role-limited access         |\n| CAP-F09  | Evacuation centre audit                                      | SOP 9-08        | Proposed                              |\n\nAnnexes: **9-A** IAT and master requirements traceability; **9-B** competency matrix by target group; **9-C** exercise plan outline; **9-D** evacuation centre audit checklist.\n\n## 18. Relationship With Other Chapters\n\n**Table 9.29**\n\n*Links Between Chapter 9 and the Other Chapters*\n\n| **Ch.** | **Title**                                                                 | **Link**                                                                                                                                    |\n|---------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 1       | Legal Mandate, DRRM Governance and Institutional Accountability           | The CDRRMC approves the training and exercise programmes and accredits volunteers.                                                          |\n| 2       | CDRRMO Organisational Structure, Staffing and HR Management               | Develops CDRRMO staff (HR-04) and sets HR rules for volunteers; this chapter folds staff needs into the City plan.                          |\n| 3       | Risk Intelligence, Planning and Institutional Preparedness                | Supplies hazards, scenarios and contingency plans for messages and exercises; reviews barangay plans.                                       |\n| 4       | Early Warning, EOC Activation, Incident Management and Emergency Response | Issues warnings and notices that the IEC programme prepares people for; runs evacuation; tasks volunteers.                                  |\n| 5       | Recovery, Rehabilitation and Build Back Better                            | Uses trained PDNA and recovery teams; recovery communication follows the risk communication plan.                                           |\n| 6       | DRRM Financing, Resource Management and Accountability                    | Funds training, exercises and EC retrofits; uses rotated stocks in drills.                                                                  |\n| 7       | Monitoring, Evaluation, Knowledge Management and Continuous Improvement   | Evaluates exercises; holds the CAPA register; reports reach and functionality in the annual results report.                                 |\n| 8       | Stakeholder Engagement and Institutional Partnerships                     | Brings partners, schools, hospitals and neighbouring LGUs into trainings and exercises.                                                     |\n| 10      | Institutional Sustainability, Deep Resilience and Recognition             | Uses training, exercise and community records as evidence for Gawad KALASAG and SGLG, and plans for capacity that outlasts terms of office. |\n\n## 19. Concluding Notes\n\nThe City already trains, drills and informs, and it has put BHERTs, volunteers and school partnerships into ordinance and resolution. What it lacks is the evidence that these activities produce capacity, and the rhythm that keeps capacity from draining away: a needs analysis, a refresher date for every skill, a progression of exercises, a functionality check for every barangay, a reach target for every message, and evacuation centres that meet the standard.\n\nWriting these procedures does not build capacity. Until the first combined training plan has been delivered with competence records, the first EOC functional exercise has been evaluated, all 85 BDRRMCs have been assessed and the evacuation centre audit has begun, this chapter remains a controlled working draft.\n\n## References\n\nThe official text governs.\n\nCity Government of Ormoc. (2023). *Ormoc City disaster risk reduction and management ten-year plan (2023–2032)*.\n\nCity Government of Ormoc. (2026a). *Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC institutional assessment tool* (Version 1.7) \\[Unpublished assessment workbook\\].\n\nCity Government of Ormoc. (2026b). *Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC master requirements traceability and 140-indicator data dictionary* (Version 1.2) \\[Unpublished project reference workbook\\].\n\nCity Government of Ormoc, City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2022–2025). *Resolution Nos. 03–04, s. 2022; Nos. 07 and 09, s. 2024; No. 04, s. 2025*.\n\nCity Government of Ormoc, Sangguniang Panlungsod. (2023–2025). *Ordinance Nos. 135, s. 2023; 160 and 171, s. 2024; 189, s. 2025; Resolution No. 2023-347*.\n\nDepartment of the Interior and Local Government. (2025). *Memorandum Circular No. 2025-035 (Listo si KAP)* \\[as cited in IAT v1.7\\].\n\nMeadows, D. H. (2008). *Thinking in systems: A primer* (D. Wright, Ed.). Chelsea Green.\n\nNational Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2012). *Memorandum Circular No. 4, s. 2012: Implementing guidelines on the use of the Incident Command System*.\n\nNational Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (n.d.). *Disaster preparedness minimum standards* (Vol. 2).\n\nNational Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2026). *Implementing rules and regulations of R